WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.783 --> 00:00:04.255
(sparse violin notes)

00:00:04.255 --> 00:00:06.111
- Imagine you're a sailor,

00:00:06.111 --> 00:00:08.160
working on a Great Lakes freighter

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.500
for two months at a time and
you need your mail delivered.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.197
Well, that's where we come in.

00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:18.114
(accordion pulses)

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:24.508
My name is Jim Hogan and I'm the owner

00:00:24.508 --> 00:00:28.886
and the fourth generation of
the J.W. Westcott Company.

00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:31.806
This company was started in 1874

00:00:31.806 --> 00:00:35.490
by my great-grandfather,
Captain John Ward Westcott.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.157
We operate a shore-to-
ship and ship-to-shore

00:00:40.354 --> 00:00:43.956
delivery boat in the Port of Detroit.

00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:46.080
We're the only floating zip code in

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.129
actually the United States
and it still holds true today.

00:00:50.129 --> 00:00:53.499
Any package that's got that 48222 zip code

00:00:53.499 --> 00:00:56.413
is going to arrive here at our station.

00:00:56.413 --> 00:00:58.590
On any given day, we receive mail

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.466
and packages in the morning.

00:01:00.466 --> 00:01:03.091
You see the various pigeon
holes, as they were called.

00:01:03.091 --> 00:01:05.156
Each of those represent different ships.

00:01:05.156 --> 00:01:08.651
We place mail in those slots and we wait

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:09.891
'till the vessel is actually coming

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:12.347
through the Port of Detroit.

00:01:12.347 --> 00:01:14.429
- [Radio] Hey Dark Dan,
how you doin' today?

00:01:14.429 --> 00:01:15.875
- Pretty good, how 'bout yourself?

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:18.565
- [Radio] Oh not too bad,
just getting finished up here.

00:01:18.565 --> 00:01:20.895
- Okay real good, we'll
see you in a little bit.

00:01:20.895 --> 00:01:22.019
(radio beeps)

00:01:22.019 --> 00:01:26.484
- We pack the mail bags,
Captain takes the vessel

00:01:26.484 --> 00:01:30.317
away from our dock and
off to Midstream we go.

00:01:31.152 --> 00:01:33.105
We deliver mail by the pail.

00:01:33.105 --> 00:01:36.271
That's the sailor's mailbox if you will.

00:01:36.271 --> 00:01:39.517
The crewman aboard the ship
will lower down a bucket

00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:41.888
and we'll fill the bucket with the mail

00:01:41.888 --> 00:01:44.705
and then we send a bucket back up.

00:01:44.705 --> 00:01:48.215
Sometimes we're delivering
passengers, crew.

00:01:48.215 --> 00:01:50.898
We had a grocery order
a number of years ago.

00:01:50.898 --> 00:01:53.007
The last item was about
three and a half feet long,

00:01:53.007 --> 00:01:57.133
I looked at it and it
was a tuna, a whole tuna.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:59.361
We end the delivery with a salute.

00:01:59.361 --> 00:02:00.565
(boat horn blows)

00:02:00.565 --> 00:02:04.520
We head back to the dock, we
wait for our next adventure.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.661
I've worked here for 43 years.

00:02:06.661 --> 00:02:08.481
I'm grateful to have
been a part of all this

00:02:08.481 --> 00:02:11.000
and I look forward to a fifth generation

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.615
taking this on and seeing where it goes,

00:02:14.615 --> 00:02:17.039
up or down the river, as it were.

00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:19.706
(violin pulses)

